  "body": "Right now, [forbidden host code points](https://url.spec.whatwg.org/#forbidden-host-code-point) only includes U+0000 NULL and ASCII whitespaces from the C0 controls range. However, there seems to be no good reason to allow the other unprintable characters. Right now according to spec, `new URL('https://a\\u0002b.com')` parses with `u.hostname === 'a\\u0002b.com'`, which deviates from what a user might expect from a URL parser.\r\n\r\nMost implementations agree with this assessment:\r\n\r\n* Chrome and Go forbid C0 controls and `\\u007F`, whether encoded or not\r\n* Firefox forbids C0 controls verbatim, as well as `%00`–`%1F` and `%7F`. It allows `\\u007F`.\r\n* curl forbids C0 controls and `\\u007F`, as well as `%00`–`%1F`. It allows `%7F`.\r\n  * This is the dual of Firefox's behavior.\r\n* Safari follows current spec.\r\n* Python and Ruby accepts any percent-encoded characters in hosts\r\n* Node.js legacy parser breaks when it sees any control character (percent-encoded or not) in hosts (it tries to add a `/` before the character or `%`)\r\n\r\nI propose Chrome's behavior as it seems more consistent than Firefox or curl.",
